  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research the serial memory/SIMD kernel. Replace separately allocated rows with contiguous buffers and eliminate the interior copy by swapping old/new buffers after each completed sweep. Both buffers must carry the original frozen boundary values, including tiny grids whose heated cell is on a boundary. Preserve zero-step output and always print the current buffer. Explore alignment, row pointers, non-aliasing annotations and vectorizable integer-OR convergence reduction. Preserve the original neighbor-addition order and double precision; do not enable reassociation or approximate arithmetic. Compare copy elimination, layout and vectorization separately using unchanged end-to-end measurements.",
        "B": "Research CPU parallelism on at most four cores using facilities supported by the fixed build. Compare one, two and four threads, favoring a persistent worker team with static contiguous row partitions over repeated thread creation. Each sweep needs a race-free OR reduction of the exact error predicate, completion of all writes before swapping buffers, and shared termination decisions before another sweep begins. Investigate a runtime work-size cutoff for serial execution when synchronization dominates; derive it from measurements rather than recognizing test inputs. Keep initialization and output deterministic, and compare both against baseline and the best available serial candidate.",
        "C": "Research exact work avoidance and output overhead as separate candidates. The initially nonzero region is known from runtime size: on sweep t, starting at t=1, no interior cell outside its rectangle expanded by t cells can become nonzero. Maintain initialized-zero inactive regions and correct frozen boundaries; compute the expanding rectangle until it reaches the full interior. Omitted cells must have provably zero update and error, without skipping any convergence decision. Separately compare larger stdout buffering and bounded row/block formatting followed by fwrite, retaining the original libc numeric conversion, spaces and newlines. Measure these variants independently; either can lose when the active region quickly fills or formatting dominates.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates that pass the unchanged oracle. Keep the fastest standalone program as a fallback, then measure selected combinations of A's kernel, B's threading, and C's active-region/output variants; gains are not additive. Reassess thread cutoffs after integration because faster kernels and smaller active regions change synchronization costs. Audit both buffers' borders, final-buffer selection, zero iterations, tiny grids, strict threshold comparison, and termination after the first converged sweep. Preserve every output value and its formatting. Select by measured whole-program performance within existing budgets, recording correctness and timings for each integration rather than assuming the most elaborate combination wins.",
        "analysis": "The supplied implementation is Problem E, Jacobi heat diffusion. This run provides only a correct baseline: four checked cases and a 0.072824-second mean across three similar samples. There is no hotspot or input-size breakdown. Plausible costs are stencil memory traffic, the extra copy sweep, and per-cell formatted output. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. All research must preserve actual source semantics, not the contradictory comment: synchronous updates, unchanged boundaries, and stopping after a completed sweep when no absolute change exceeds the float-literal threshold 1.0e-5f, or when the iteration limit is reached. Preserve inclusive initialization and the six-decimal output with trailing spaces and row newlines. Do not alter the compiler, evaluator, tests, budgets or target, or specialize to measured inputs."
      },
      "request_sha256": "7da5926e695a4233b60ff03cf6741c88824005a3a454dd5718af94002c797c2a",
