    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop the storage/locality donor: contiguous double buffers, local row pointers and valid restrict contracts, compatible with C's persistent team. C already removes the copy, so measure the additional value of contiguous storage separately. Keep the simple single-sweep implementation available. Treat two-sweep row caching as optional: its observed improvement over simple contiguous ping-pong is only about 0.7%. Any temporal variant must preserve per-sweep convergence, recover the first-sweep result when necessary, handle odd limits, and provide thread-private caches with correct halo calculations.",
        "B": "Develop a transplantable ARM64 SIMD row kernel from caec810f8c8147d9: horizontal-vector reuse, eight-cell unrolling and independent Boolean accumulators. Compare it with the simpler paired-cell and restrict-only kernels within the same no-copy host program. Preserve the original floating-point addition sequence and float-rounded convergence threshold. Make accumulators private to each worker and reset them every sweep; combine scalar tails into the same convergence decision. Keep loads and stores within row bounds, retain the scalar fallback, and do not transplant B's obsolete copy pass.",
        "C": "Use 4f83638c90194c8e as the threaded reference. Research workload-based serial/team selection and code-level team sizes up to the four available cores, under the unchanged build and execution environment. Compare against the existing side-length-128 gate; the slower candidate's 4096-cells-per-thread gate is only a hypothesis. Preserve persistent static row ownership, private buffer pointers and the one-barrier, double-banked convergence protocol. Do not collapse the flag banks without synchronization proving that readers cannot race with reuse. Retain correct serial and non-OpenMP paths.",
        "G": "Preserve 4f83638c90194c8e as the incumbent. Prioritize transplanting B's best SIMD kernel into it, then evaluate A's simple contiguous layout separately and in combination as the existing budget permits. Measure every complete integration; neither parent gains nor rankings are additive. Integrate improved C scheduling independently where possible, and defer temporal blocking until its convergence recovery and threaded halos are sound. Use unchanged validation and end-to-end measurements. Audit tiny/no-interior grids, unchanged hot boundary cells, zero and odd iteration limits, convergence exits, SIMD tails, buffer parity and flag-bank races. Select the fastest validated whole program, retaining the incumbent if combinations regress.",
        "analysis": "The measured baseline is 72.82 ms. C's ping-pong/persistent-team package leads at 47.46 ms, followed by C's copying variant at 48.72 ms. A reaches 59.83/60.28 ms and B 63.18/65.57 ms. No mixed integration is measured. A's copy elimination overlaps the winning C program, while its temporal-blocking increment is small; B's SIMD remains a plausible complementary donor. These are end-to-end results, not a measured bottleneck breakdown. All roles must preserve synchronous double-precision Jacobi, the original addition grouping, strict error > (double)1.0e-5f, the first converged sweep, iteration limits, frozen initial boundaries and exact output formatting. Initialize both swapped buffers with those boundaries, including nonzero boundaries on tiny grids. Keep every valid input correct without changing the compiler, target, tests, oracle or budgets."
      },
